    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Modify the code below by using the <c>myscore</c> function to get the training error for each possible model of size <m>k=2</m>.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  results = []
                  for variables in itertools.combinations(X.columns, 2):
                      score = myscore(...)
                      results.append((variables, score))
                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use <c>itertools.combinations</c> to enumerate every unordered pair of predictor names.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>For each pair, select those two columns, call <c>myscore</c>, and store both the pair and its training error.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not use permutations: <c>{A, B}</c> and <c>{B, A}</c> define the same model and should be evaluated only once.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  def getBestModel_df(df, k=2, outputvar = 'mpg'):
                      '''
                      This function will return the best model for a given number of input variables
                      '''
                      myvars = []
                      myscores = []

                      if k == 0:

                          return pd.DataFrame({'Vars':'null model', 'Train Score':[myscore(auto,'null model')]})
                      else:

                          for Xs in combinations(inputvars,k):
                              myvars.append(Xs) 

                              myscores.append(myscore(auto,Xs)) #&lt;--- Fix this None

                          myResults = pd.DataFrame({'Vars':myvars, 'Train Score':myscores})
                          return myResults

                  print("k = 2")
                  print(getBestModel_df(auto, k=2))
                  print("\n")
                  print("k = 0, so this is the null model")
                  print(getBestModel_df(auto, k=0))
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is <m>M_2</m>? That is, what is the set of variables that give the lowest training error over all sets of size 2? Write a command to pull this automatically from the table above.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>the command <c>DataFrame.idxmin()</c> will give you the row index of the minimum entry in a pandas series.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Locate the row with the minimum training-error value in the size-two results table.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Use <c>idxmin()</c> on the score column, then use <c>.loc</c> to retrieve the corresponding variable set.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> <m>M_2</m> is the best model among those with exactly two predictors, not the two individual predictors with the smallest one-variable errors.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  def getBestModel(df, k=2, outputvar = 'mpg'):
                      '''
                      This function will return the best model for a given number of input variables
                      '''
                      myResults = getBestModel_df(df, k, outputvar)

                      # Find the index of the minimum training score
                      indexmin = myResults['Train Score'].idxmin()
                      M_k = myResults.Vars[indexmin]
                      return M_k

                  print(getBestModel(auto, k=2))

                  # Check to make sure this is right :-P
                  getBestModel(auto, k=2) == ('horsepower', 'weight')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Put it all together. Finish the for loop that runs through <m>k=0</m> to <m>k=5</m>, and gets the set of variables giving the best size <m>k</m> model, and then computs the test error for each model. Which model would be returned by the best subset selection method?</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  best_models = []
                  test_errors = []
                  for k in range(0, 6):
                      # Find the lowest-training-error subset of size k.
                      best_variables = ...
                      # Refit/evaluate that selected model on the test set.
                      test_error = ...
                      best_models.append(best_variables)
                      test_errors.append(test_error)

                  ...
                </code>
              </program>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>For each size <m>k</m>, first search all size-<m>k</m> subsets using training data only and keep the subset with minimum training error.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Then compare the selected <m>M_k</m> models using test error and choose the model size with the smallest test error.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not use the test set to choose the best subset within each size and then reuse it to report performance; that leaks test information into model selection.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  for k in range(5):
                      M_k = getBestModel(auto, k)
                      print(f"Best {k} variable model: {M_k}")
                      M_k_CV = myscore_cv(auto,M_k)
                      print(f"CV Score: {M_k_CV:0.3f}")

                  # The best model is the 2 variable model using horsepower and weight
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
